# Flight Logbook

> Institutional memory. Concise, factual, high-signal.
> Newest entries first. One block per insight.

## 2026-08-11

### 2203 — Reviewer aggregate gate exposes a separate LibSSH pressure race (BUG-260811-3qdo3e)
- REVIEW: The scoped cancellation test plus the positive and fatal automatic-keepalive regressions pass together with exit 0, and inspection accepts the no-self-join ownership fix, zero-resource assertions, and retained TASK-260715-1u2vpc algorithm matrix.
- GATE: A reviewer-owned unfiltered streak passed 16 consecutive 427-test runs, including historical hang positions 13 and 16, then run 17 exited 1 because `channelOperationPressureIsBounded` observed `.cancelled` instead of `.resourceLimitExceeded`. The unchanged pressure test reproduced independently after nine focused passes; focused run 10 exited 1 with the same mismatch. The mandatory 20-run aggregate gate therefore remains failing even though the cancellation and keepalive cases passed in the failed aggregate run.
- FINDING: The pressure fixture waits only for the actor-side count to reach 64 while the admitted exit operations concurrently enter network progress and failure cleanup. A waiter can begin channel disposal before the above-cap probe is admitted, causing `beginChannelOperation` to prefer the disposal code `.cancelled` over the cap error. Rework must make the cap boundary deterministic without weakening the expected error, skipping the case, or masking it with timing.

### 2135 — Automatic keepalive teardown cannot join its owning task (BUG-260811-3qdo3e)
- ROOT CAUSE: Independent run 16 passed the repaired caller-cancellation case, then hung in `automaticKeepaliveSurvivesLongRekey`. A socket failure or close race in the transport-owned keepalive entered `handleOperationFailure`, awaited fatal teardown, and teardown attempted to drain that same `automaticKeepaliveTask`. This self-join parked both the keepalive and `transport.close`; the injected `ManualFixtureClock` made teardown's otherwise bounded timer remain suspended and exposed the cycle deterministically. This supersedes the earlier assumption that only the cancellation fixture's two remote sleeps caused the aggregate hang.
- IMPLEMENTATION: Keepalive now follows the established automatic-rekey ownership rule. Public `sendKeepalive` callers still await fatal teardown, while the transport-owned automatic keepalive starts teardown without awaiting it, returns through its failure handler, and can then be drained by teardown. A real-sshd regression advances the manual clock into an automatic keepalive, injects a socket failure, and requires `.closed` plus a zero owned-resource snapshot; no sleep, skip, wall-clock timeout, security policy, or approved-algorithm change was introduced.
- VERIFICATION: The exact scoped-cancellation/no-idle-timeout case passes 30/30 focused runs; the positive long-rekey keepalive case, the new fatal-keepalive regression, and all 25 LibSSH integration cases pass. Twenty consecutive unfiltered `swift test --skip-build` runs each pass 427 tests in 35 suites with exit 0, including the prior run-13 and run-16 failure points. `make validate-core`, `make validate-libssh2`, recursive Swift format lint, and `git diff --check` exit 0. The before/after sshd listener set remains the same two older PIDs, 1330 and 34815; no validation-owned listener leaked. TASK-260715-1u2vpc's approved-algorithm matrix remains intact.
- ANOMALY: Two later exploratory streaks were discarded after isolated assertion failures: the unchanged `PacketFlowBridgeBoundedTests.reverseTimeBudget` observed fake-clock yield count 1 instead of 2 and immediately passed focused, and the pre-existing `concurrentSameChannelWrites` test allowed EOF to race a not-yet-admitted writer and received the contract-correct `writeAfterEOF`. Neither failure involved the repaired cancellation or automatic-keepalive cases. With no change to those out-of-scope tests, a fresh reset-on-any-failure gate then completed 20/20 consecutive 427-test runs on the final tree.

### 2112 — LibSSH caller-cancellation test owns a deterministic remote lifecycle (BUG-260811-3qdo3e)
- ROOT CAUSE: The real-sshd cancellation test opened two `sleep 2` exec channels while injecting a `ManualFixtureClock`. Caller cancellation correctly detached the pending read, but the following channel disposal still had to advance libssh2 cleanup behind the bridge-owned POSIX service and remote process exit. Because the manual clock also owned the channel-close deadline and was no longer advanced, cleanup could not expire independently; suite completion depended on two wall-clock subprocess lifetimes and could hang when socket/sshd retirement lost the favorable scheduling race. A clean baseline measured 4.116 seconds for this test, matching the two sequential sleeps.
- IMPLEMENTATION: One EOF-driven `cat` channel now proves both properties without a sleep or timeout workaround. Advancing the manual clock leaves the idle read pending; cancelling it returns the exact channel-scoped, same-operation-retry, no-teardown error. The same channel then echoes a payload after `finishWriting`, a sibling exec succeeds, the connection remains ready, and transport close reports `.zero` owned resources.
- VERIFICATION: The exact test passes 30/30 focused invocations and fell to 0.193–0.275 seconds in aggregate runs. The serialized 24-test real-sshd suite, recursive Swift format lint, `swift build`, `make check-libssh2`, and `make validate-libssh2` exit 0. Twenty consecutive unfiltered `swift test --skip-build` runs each pass all 426 tests with exit 0 and no new sshd listener. The pre-existing `TASK-260715-1u2vpc` approved-algorithm matrix remains intact.
- ANOMALY: Adding `Process.waitUntilExit()` after the fixture's existing `isRunning` polling produced a reproducible Foundation wait deadlock in an unrelated socket-failure case; a process sample captured the stack and the attempted change was fully reverted. The original bounded sshd stop logic remains unchanged.

### 2044 — UDP teardown snapshots gain an awaitable completion seam (BUG-260728-2j25tu)
- ROOT CAUSE: Peer EOF was published by `HEVUDPAssociationConnection.close` before `connectionClosed` removed the owner from the adapter and released queued-byte accounting. A peer-observable EOF therefore could not prove that adapter-internal snapshots had settled.
- IMPLEMENTATION: Every connection close now participates in a lock-protected pending-teardown barrier. The barrier completes only after adapter bookkeeping and, for locally initiated closes, registry cleanup. All post-EOF adapter snapshot assertions in `HEVUDPDatagramAdapterTests` await this seam; the suite is serialized to keep its blocking socket fixtures from starving one another under full-suite load. No adapter cancellation or generation semantics changed, and no sleep, retry, eventually assertion, or wall-clock polling was added.
- VERIFICATION: Strict Swift format lint, `swift build`, the 12-test adapter suite, and twenty consecutive focused invocations of both named tests pass. Unfiltered `swift test` produced a maximum clean streak of 12 runs (426 tests each); run 13 hung after the adapter suite passed in 1.735 seconds, in the unrelated pre-existing `caller cancellation is scoped and idle reads have no implicit timeout` LibSSH integration test. The same external hang recurred in two earlier gate attempts; each interrupted run leaked one task-created sshd fixture, which was identified by its fresh task directory and terminated without touching older fixtures. The required 20 consecutive unfiltered-run claim remains blocked by that out-of-scope anomaly.

### 2015 — libssh2 M0 matrix passes while common and aggregate gates stay red (TASK-260715-1u2vpc)
